{%- doc -%}
  Gives the Appstle subscription widget the surface it never paints for itself.

  Appstle is not colouring its text wrongly: it honours `--color-foreground`. The trouble is where
  it is rendered. `div.product-information.section` carries a colour scheme that resolves
  `--color-foreground` to the cream `#f4f3e9` against a `--color-background` of tan, inverted from
  the `#470a12` the page resolves, and nothing in that subtree ever paints the tan. So the widget's
  text arrives cream and lands on the page's own cream, at a measured contrast of 1.00. Its two
  inner surfaces make it worse: `appstle_loyalty_text` paints itself white, and the link strip
  paints Appstle's brand blue under white text, at 2.63.

  Rather than reach into that scheme - which would change the whole product page - the widget is
  given a surface of its own, so it is legible on any ground it is dropped onto. The pair comes
  from `:root`, captured into properties no section overrides: a custom property declared on
  `:root` computes there and inherits its computed value, so these hold the page's `#470a12` on
  `#f4f3e9` even inside a section that has inverted the tokens they were read from. That is the
  pairing the add to cart button and the prices already use, and it measures 14.27.

  The pills invert the same pair, which is the theme's own treatment for
  `.purchase-options__badge` - ink ground, paper text.

  `!important` is deliberate and load-bearing: Appstle injects its CSS at runtime from its app
  embed, so it lands after the theme bundle and wins at equal specificity. Measured on the live
  product page - every class below moves from 1.00/1.11/2.63 to 14.27.

  @example
  {% render 'appstle-widget-styles' %}
{%- enddoc -%}

{% stylesheet %}
  /* Captured at :root, where the brand pair is still the brand pair. Sections further down
     redefine --color-foreground and --color-background; these two keep the page's own values
     because a custom property resolves where it is declared, not where it is used. */
  :root {
    /* Literal fallbacks are not decoration. If the theme ever moves these tokens off :root onto a
       scheme class, var(--color-foreground) here becomes invalid at computed-value time, both
       properties below resolve to unset, and the widget silently returns to cream on cream with
       no error anywhere. The fallbacks are settings.color_palette's own values. */
    --ona-panel-surface: var(--color-background, #f4f3e9);
    --ona-panel-ink: var(--color-foreground, #470a12);
  }

  /* The widget and the reward table both paint their own ground, so neither depends on whatever
     is behind them. appstle_loyalty_text ships painted white, which is why it is named here. */
  .appstle_sub_widget,
  .appstle_sub_widget .appstle_loyalty_text {
    background-color: var(--ona-panel-surface) !important;
    color: var(--ona-panel-ink) !important;
  }

  /* Appstle sets these individually, so inheriting the widget's colour is not enough. */
  .appstle_sub_widget .appstle_loyalty_title,
  .appstle_sub_widget .appstle_tooltip_title,
  .appstle_sub_widget .appstle-loyalty-table-data,
  .appstle_sub_widget .appstle-loyalty-billing-cycle,
  .appstle_sub_widget .appstle-precentage-loyalty {
    color: var(--ona-panel-ink) !important;
  }

  /* The strip behind the two links ships in Appstle's brand blue, which belongs to no scheme on
     this site. Dropped, so the pills below read against the widget's own surface. */
  .appstle_sub_widget .appstle_tooltip_appstle {
    background-color: transparent !important;
  }

  /* Inverted, the same way .purchase-options__badge is: the button takes the ink as its ground
     and the surface as its text. */
  .appstle_sub_widget .appstle_link,
  .appstle_sub_widget .appstle_subcription_link {
    background-color: var(--ona-panel-ink) !important;
    color: var(--ona-panel-surface) !important;
    border-radius: var(--style-border-radius-pills);
  }
{% endstylesheet %}
